 ## Background
-The concept of exploiting temporal coherence in volume rendering originated in the late 1990s and early 2000s, with early works focusing on C-Buffer techniques to accelerate ray casting by reusing hit distances [S3, unofficial]. The introduction of 4D data rendering led to caching strategies that store entire time-series in GPU memory to avoid PCIe bottlenecks [S1, unofficial]. More recently, with the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) and Implicit Neural Representations (INRs), temporal coherence has been adapted to dynamic octree expansion and multi-resolution GPU caching to handle large-scale interactive visualization [S2, S1, unofficial]. These methods are widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and interactive fly-throughs of large datasets where frame rates are critical [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
